# Compatibility policy for the release bundles, read by .github/workflows/release-firmware.yml.
#
# Domesday Duplicator - LaserDisc RF sampler
# SPDX-FileCopyrightText: 2026 Simon Inns
# SPDX-License-Identifier: GPL-3.0-or-later
#
# These are decisions, not facts, which is why they are a tracked file rather than something
# a workflow derives. The file is part of the tagged commit, so a tag pins the policy the
# same way it pins the source. Everything else in the manifest — the payload digests, the
# protocol version the firmware advertises, the register map version the gateware reports —
# is read out of the sources at release time, because those *are* facts and a workflow that
# restated them could restate them wrongly.

# The oldest capture application (gui-v* stream) permitted to install a bundle built from
# this commit. The application refuses the install outright below this and says "update the
# application first" — a user cannot use one version of the application to flash a device
# past what that same version understands.
#
# Deliberately a floor of 0.0.0, meaning "no application version is too old", until there is
# a numbered application release to name. Getting this wrong upwards is the expensive
# direction: a bundle demanding a version that was never published refuses to install on
# every machine, and the file it has to be fixed in is inside a tag that has already
# shipped. Getting it wrong downwards costs nothing here, because the checks that carry the
# real compatibility weight are machine-to-machine and always enforced: the firmware's
# protocol version in bcdDevice and the gateware's register map version at register 0x01,
# both compared against the ranges the running application was built knowing.
#
# Raise this the moment a bundle needs an application newer than one already in the field —
# in the same commit that makes it true.
MINIMUM_APPLICATION_VERSION=0.0.0
